notes.front:
**Fix formatId(id)**

This doesn't compile: `toUpperCase` doesn't exist on numbers. Narrow the union: numbers become 6 digits with leading zeros (`42` → `"000042"`), strings are upper-cased.

```ts
function formatId(id: number | string): string {
  return id.toUpperCase();
}
```

notes.back:
// Why: After `typeof id === "number"` returns, TypeScript knows id is a string on the next
// line.
// Docs: https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/2/everyday-types.html
function formatId(id: number | string): string {
  if (typeof id === "number") return String(id).padStart(6, "0");
  return id.toUpperCase();
}

notes.starter_code:
function formatId(id: number | string): string {
  return id.toUpperCase();
}

notes.front:
**describeInput(value)**

Write `describeInput(value: string | string[] | null): string` — `"nothing"` for null, `"text: <value>"` for a string, `"list of <n>"` for an array.

```ts
function describeInput(value: string | string[] | null): string {
  return "text: " + value;
}
```

notes.back:
// Why: Check null and arrays first; whatever is left must be a string. An empty string is
// not null.
// Docs: https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/2/narrowing.html
function describeInput(value: string | string[] | null): string {
  if (value === null) return "nothing";
  if (Array.isArray(value)) return `list of ${value.length}`;
  return `text: ${value}`;
}

notes.starter_code:
function describeInput(value: string | string[] | null): string {
  return "text: " + value;
}

notes.front:
**Type guard isString**

`isString` works at run time, but `["a", 1].filter(isString)` still has the type `(string | number)[]`. Change its return type to a type predicate so the filter result is `string[]`.

```ts
function isString(value: unknown): boolean {
  return typeof value === "string";
}
```

notes.back:
// Why: `value is string` tells TypeScript that true means "value is a string", and filter
// uses that to narrow its result. The compiler does not check that your body really tests
// for a string: a wrong guard lies to it.
// Docs: https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/2/narrowing.html
function isString(value: unknown): value is string {
  return typeof value === "string";
}

notes.starter_code:
function isString(value: unknown): boolean {
  return typeof value === "string";
}

